Let  $p$  be a real number and  $f(x)=x^p-x+p$ . Prove that:

(a) Every root  $\alpha$  of  $f(x)$  satisfies  $|\alpha|<p^{\frac1{p-1}}$ ;
(b) If  $p$  is a prime number, then  $f(x)$  cannot be written as the product of two non-constant polynomials with integer coefficients.

Let  $x,y,z$  be pairwise coprime positive integers and  $p\ge5$  and  $q$  be prime numbers which satisfy the following conditions:

(i)  $6p$  does not divide  $q-1$ ;
(ii)  $q$  divides  $x^2+xy+y^2$ ;
(iii)  $q$  does not divide  $x+y-z$ .

Prove that  $x^p+y^p\ne z^p$ .

Prove that for every prime number  $p\ge5$ ,

(a)  $p^3$  divides  $\binom{2p}p-2$ ;
(b)  $p^3$  divides  $\binom{kp}p-k$  for every natural number  $k$ .

Find all functions  $f$  , defined and having values in the set of integer numbers, for which the following conditions are satisfied:
(a)  $f(1) = 1$ ;
(b) for every two whole (integer) numbers  $m$  and  $n$ , the following equality is satisfied:  $$ f(m+n)·(f(m)-f(n)) = f(m-n)·(f(m)+ f(n)) $$

