Cute.  Here's an inductive solution:

<details><summary>Click to expand</summary>Forget for a moment that we know some values for the  $ x_i$ .  I claim that the given expression is equal to  $ \frac {1}{x_1\cdot x_2 \cdots x_n}$ .  The proof is inductive:  it's clearly true for  $ n = 1$ .  Let  $ S(k)$  be the set of permutations of  $ \{x_1, \ldots, x_n\}$  that end in  $ k$  and let  $ T(k)$  be the set of permutations of  $ \{x_1, \ldots, x_n\} \setminus \{x_k\}$ .  Then for  $ n \geq 2$ ,
\begin{eqnarray*} \sum_\sigma 1/Q(\sigma) & =& \sum_k \sum_{\sigma \in S(k)} 1/Q(\sigma) 
& = &\sum_k \sum_{\sigma \in T(k)} \frac {1}{Q(\sigma)(x_1 + \ldots + x_n)} 
& = &\frac {1}{x_1 + \ldots + x_n} \sum_{k} \sum_{\sigma \in T(k)} 1/Q(\sigma) 
& = &\frac {1}{x_1 + \ldots + x_n} \sum_{k} \frac {x_k}{x_1 \cdots x_n} 
& = &\frac {1}{x_1 \cdots x_n} \end{eqnarray*}
where we apply the inductive hypothesis going from the third to the fourth line.</details>

The best path for the particle to travel is to head to a point  $(p,0), 0 \leq p \leq 2$  via the  $x$  axis. Fix the  $x-$ axis of all such accessible points. Note that if our particle departs  $(p,0)$ , it can only move at most  $\frac{2-p}{p}$  meters. Hence, we have  $(x-p)^2 + y^2 \leq (\frac{2-p}{p})^2$   $$ \implies y^2 \leq \frac{3}{4} (\frac{4}{9} (2-x)^2 - (p - \frac{4x-2}{3})^2) $$   $y^2$  is maximized when  $p = \frac{2(2x-1)}{3}$  if  $\frac{1}{2} \leq x \leq 2$  which implies  $$ y \leq \frac{\sqrt{3}}{3}(2-x) $$   $\frac{1}{2} \leq x \leq 2$ .  $y^2$  is maximized when  $p = 0$  if  $0 \leq x \leq \frac{1}{2}$  which implies  $$ y^2 \leq 1-x^2 $$  Hence, our region is
