We show that  $P$  is indeed, the incenter of  $WXYZ$ . $\rightarrow $  Firstly, as  $PX \perp \overline{BC} , PY \perp \overline{AB} \implies WBXP$  is cyclic. Similarly,  $XPYC, YPZD, ZPWA$  are all cyclic.

Thus,  $\angle WXP = \angle WBP = \angle ABD = \angle ACD = \angle PCY = \angle PXY$ .
Hence,  $PX$  bisects  $\angle WXY$ . Analogously, the other angles are bisected as well. Hence,  $P$  is the incenter of  $WXYZ$ , as desired.

Thus, by Pithot's theorem, we have:  $WX +YZ = XY + ZW \ \blacksquare$

The sequence  $\big\{f(n)\big\}_{n =1}^\infty$  is a nondecreasing sequence of positive integers, arranged such a way that the integer  $m$  occurs  $s+1$  times, where  $m=2^s t$ , where  $t$  is odd.

We can prove the conclusion by induction on s: (1) When s=0, it suffice to show that  $f(n)=t$ (where t is odd) has a unique solution. Since  $f(n+1)=f\big(n+1-f(n)\big)+f\big(n-f(n-1)\big)$ , which implies that  $f\big(n+1-f(n)\big)=\dfrac{t+1}{2}$ , 
this implies  $f(n+2)\neq t$ , otherwise, we can obtain  $f\big(n+1-f(n)\big)=\dfrac{t-1}{2}$  similarily,  which is a contradiction!

(2) Suppose  $$ \left\{
\begin{array}{l}
f(n)=2^s\cdot t-1
f(n+1)=2^s\cdot t
\cdots
f(n+k)=2^s\cdot t
f(n+k+1)=2^s\cdot t+1
\end{array}
\right. $$  Then, we can obtain the equations  $$ \left\{
\begin{array}{l}
f\big(n-f(n-2)\big)=2^{s-1}\cdot t-1
f\big(n+2-f(n+2)\big)=\cdots=f\big(n+k-f(n+k-1)\big))=2^{s-1}
f\big(n+k+1-f(n+k)\big)=2^{s-1}\cdot t+1
\end{array}
\right. $$  Observe that  $n-f(n-2),n+2-f(n+1),\cdots,n+k+1-f(n+k)$  are the consecutive integers, then by induction,  $k-1=s$  hence  $k=s+1$ .

