Let the intersection of  $DE$  and  $CF$  be  $E^{\prime}$  and let the intersection of  $AE^{\prime}$  and  $BC$  be  $G.$  Observe that it suffices to prove that  $DH$  bisects  $\angle FDE.$ Now, since  $\angle HDC=90^{\circ},$  by well-known lemma, it suffices to prove that  $(F, E^{\prime}; H, C)=-1.$  However, since  $B\in \overrightarrow{AF},$   $D\in \overrightarrow{AH},$   $G\in \overrightarrow{AE^{\prime}},$  and  $C\in \overrightarrow{AC},$  we have  $(F, E^{\prime}; H, C)\overset{A}{=}(B, G; D, C)=\dfrac{DB}{DG}\cdot \dfrac{CG}{CB}$  using directed lengths.

We now use undirected lengths. Observe that  $B\in \overleftrightarrow{HE},$   $B\in\overleftrightarrow{DC},$   $E\in \overleftrightarrow{CA},$  and  $H\in \overleftrightarrow{AD},$  so by Menelaus,  $\dfrac{DB}{BC}\cdot \dfrac{CE}{EA}\cdot \dfrac{AH}{HD}=1.$  Also, observe that  $DE,$   $AG,$  and  $CH$  concur with  $G\in \overleftrightarrow{DC},$   $E\in \overleftrightarrow{CA},$  and  $H\in \overleftrightarrow{AD},$  so by Ceva,  $\dfrac{CG}{GD}\cdot \dfrac{DH}{HA}\cdot \dfrac{AE}{CE}=1.$  Multiplying these yields  $1=\left(\dfrac{DB}{BC}\cdot \dfrac{CE}{EA}\cdot \dfrac{AH}{HD}\right)\left(\dfrac{CG}{GD}\cdot \dfrac{DH}{HA}\cdot \dfrac{AE}{CE}\right)=\dfrac{DB}{BC}\cdot \dfrac{CG}{GD}\left(\dfrac{CE}{EA}\cdot \dfrac{AE}{CE}\right)\left(\dfrac{AH}{HD}\cdot \dfrac{DH}{HA}\right)=\dfrac{DB}{BC}\cdot \dfrac{CG}{GD}.$ We now use directed lengths again. Observe that  $\left|\dfrac{DB}{DG}\cdot \dfrac{CG}{CB}\right|=\left|\dfrac{DB}{BC}\cdot \dfrac{CG}{GD}\right|=1,$  so  $\dfrac{DB}{DG}\cdot \dfrac{CG}{CB}=\pm 1.$  However, note that  $DB$  and  $DG$  are of opposite signs and  $CG$  and  $CB$  are of the same sign, so  $\dfrac{DB}{DG}<0$  and  $\dfrac{CG}{CB}>0,$  so  $\dfrac{DB}{DG}\cdot \dfrac{CG}{CB}<0,$  so  $\dfrac{DB}{DG}\cdot \dfrac{CG}{CB}=-1.$ Now,  $(F, E^{\prime}; H, C)=(B, G; D, C)=\dfrac{DB}{DC}\cdot \dfrac{CG}{CB}=-1,$  as desired.  $\Box$

